{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources","id":1332228,"title":"How is your party using Resources?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-08-22T20:54:31-07:00","id":1332228,"title":"How is your party using Resources?","content":"My players are a wandering exorcist and a pirate captain. Through their adventures, they have come to have extremely respectable wealth- they went from 1 pirate ship to a small armada (6 ships currently) and the pirate crew has grown from 30 to over 150. They have raided treasure fleets, Fae dreamcastles, Deathlord Tributary ships, and more. Currently they have Resources 4 and i gave them a one off treasure fit for &quot;a single Resources 5 purchase each&quot;. <br /> <br /> They have bought nothing. <br /> <br /> I set the upkeep of the ships and crew at Resources 3. They have hard won artifacts already. They just don't <i>need</i> anything. They don't really <i>want</i> anything either. <br /> <br /> So, what are you using your resources on? What things can I give my players the opportunity to buy?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Please be warned: this is not champagne, this is most likely a duck.<br /> -<i>Chausse</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#800080\"><i>Message me for Japanese translations.</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8830-sorcerous-overlord","name":"Sorcerous Overlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-22T21:46:45-07:00","id":1332232,"title":"","content":"Bribes, extravagant foods, entertainment. Nothing huge, I don't think you're often likely to come up with stuff that actually tax their resources unless they need to replace or do serious repairs to some of their ships.<br /> Honestly in mine most of the group are resources 3 except the merchant who's resource 5. Granted my party are Dynasts, so they have a bit more impunity with their spending. Honestly expecting one of them to just buy a tea shop at some point just so they can have people who annoy them thrown out through the walls by their enforcer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-22T23:07:58-07:00","id":1332235,"title":"","content":"Tbh, there's not much necessary, you're mostly just going to spend it on luxuries. Like people in the real world.<br /> <br /> Mostly when I see PCs spending a lot of cash, it's buying food to feed starving countries, bribes, or paying armies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-22T23:17:05-07:00","id":1332237,"title":"","content":"Give them the opportunity to solve problems just by throwing money at things sometimes. Can they bribe another group of competing pirates into joining them? Are there any bribable heads of state that need to ignore piracy?<br /> <br /> Listen to them if they ever express a desire for a thing they can\u2019t immediately get and give them an opportunity to buy it later on.<br /> <br /> There are magical things in the setting that aren\u2019t full on artifacts that can be bought from the right person. Hell, even some minor artifacts are common enough that you could probably buy them. Does everyone have a Collar of Dawn\u2019s Cleansing Light?<br /> <br /> Do any of them have NPCs they care about? Do those NPCs live lives of luxury yet?<br /> <br /> Are there any \u201cquest maguffins\u201d they need? Can they just buy those? Maybe not all of them, but...<br /> <br /> Encourage everyone to consider the kinds of things rich people get to do in real life and whether they want to do that in game. Maybe have them meet up with a Guild Merchant Princess next time they\u2019re in a neutral port and let them see her just buy her way out of problems and petty annoyances.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-22T23:28:15-07:00","id":1332239,"title":"","content":"This is where I have found it useful to implement &quot;living standards&quot; one can choose to maintain themselves at.<br /> <br /> Among....well, arguably-most social circles, a leader is expected to keep their followers to a certain &quot;quality of life&quot;, as it were. Your household troops/loyal followers don't want to be treated like dirty peasants, and would appreciate getting better food, better living quarters and more. Likewise, if you are a leader, you are expected to maintain <i>yourself</i> to a higher standard, not just hoard wealth. And, as always, things need repairing, and repairs cost money.<br /> <br /> For example, in my mortal games a &quot;low to mid-level noble&quot; would be set at Resources 3 (I basically rewrote the Resources system). That doesn't mean they are rolling in cash. No, they have to maintain themselves, their families, their retainers, their estate, their equipment, to a standard acceptable to their peers. That costs money, and if you actually get into the nitty-gritty my players never really <i>had</i> all that much money readily available, and actually went into debt and took out loans quite a few times. They made money from their holdings, yes, but that income in turn went into their own upkeep, the upkeep of their estates, maintaining their retinues, etc, holding feasts, paying dowries, keeping up with fashion, etc. It costs a rather-large amount of capital to keep a warrior-noble fighting-fit, with adequate equipment, healthy horses, a well-armed retinue, etc etc etc<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that Resources isn't &quot;<i>money&quot;</i>, per se, it represents the wealth from a sustaining source. <br /> Most of &quot;my&quot; Resources ratings were enough to maintain a hypothetical group of people at that level for a year, with a little left over for additional expenses. Going below that &quot;maintenance level&quot; usually impacted the character.groups Influence rating, or affected them socially. If a warrior-noble has a few bad years and is left largely-destitute as a result, their good name will suffer, since they will not have the capital to pay an acceptable dowry, keep up with fashions at court, or present themselves to an acceptable standard of living as expected of nobility. Their equipment might even suffer, since they might lack the capital for regular maintenance.<br /> <br /> For example, let us examine a noble family from the Hundred Kingdoms. They are nominally rated as having Resources 3. This allows them to maintain their country manor-house, keep servants and armed soldiers, maintain a wardrobe in good fashion, eat good food regularly, put money aside for a wedding, and hold feasts every so often, and for the warrior to keep their equipment (sword, spears, shield, dagger, bow, armor and several horses, as well as several servants) in good order and fighting-fit. In addition to keeping themselves &quot;at condition&quot;, the family will also have the ability to put aside some money for expenses <i>at that Resources Ratin</i>g, allowing them to &quot;purchase&quot; goods, services and etc at that Rating-level. A new sword or a set of armor wouldnt be breaking the bank for the noble-family, so long as it wasn't every other week.<br /> <br /> However, let us see what happens when the family falls on hard times. Due to a hard winter, or a number of bad harvests, or bandit-raids on their tenants, the family has decreased capital. Now, they are rated as Resources 2 as opposed to 3. Due to a lack of funds, their manor-house starts to show signs of disrepair, they have to let servants go, their clothing starts to become out-of-fashion or even in disrepair, their food gets closer to peasant-fare than refined delicacies, and the warrior of the household to have issues with their equipment: armor and weapons rust, horses become thin, etc etc . As a result, their social status takes a hit, since they are not (cannot) maintain themselves to an acceptable standard.<br /> <br /> Essentially, if my players have a Resource Rating, I am going to assume it is getting used at, well, keeping them alive, not just sitting somewhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T01:12:12-07:00","id":1332245,"title":"","content":"Better ship.<br /> <br /> [Edit - less flippant answer]<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sorcerous Overlord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources#post1332228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, what are you using your resources on? What things can I give my players the opportunity to buy?</div> </div> </div> </div>I use Resources on the things I want. That's what you should offer. What are your characters doing and how might they use money to make their lives easier.<br /> <br /> Exorcism and pirate supplies. Salt. Rations. Bones of revered saints.<br /> <br /> New ships. Better ships.<br /> <br /> Heroic crewmembers.<br /> <br /> Exceptional equipment of all varieties. Fancy clothes. Nice weapons. Equip the crew.<br /> <br /> Maintainance. Bribes. Gifts. Ransoms.<br /> <br /> Land. Build your own port.<br /> <br /> Exotic components for Crafting and Workings. Offerings to gods.<br /> <br /> You're ST dude. You make the plot. Make being wealthy useful. Wealth can buy your way around a lot of things. Before every roll ask yourself if this is something they could pay someone else to do for them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 08-24-2019, 06:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T02:24:26-07:00","id":1332250,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sorcerous Overlord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources#post1332228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Currently they have Resources 4 and i gave them a one off treasure fit for &amp;quot;a single Resources 5 purchase each&amp;quot;. <br /> <br /> They have bought nothing. <br /> <br /> I set the upkeep of the ships and crew at Resources 3. They have hard won artifacts already. They just don't <i>need</i> anything. They don't really <i>want</i> anything either.<br /> <br /> So, what are you using your resources on? What things can I give my players the opportunity to buy?</div> </div> </div> </div>This Edition had kind of moved away from that model of &quot;having Resources allows you to make basically unlimited purchases of things with a level below your rating&quot; in favour of a greater focus on limited quantities at any given time, and handling paying of debts.<br /> <br /> Is the wealth of these characters in a form liquid enough to ensure that their crews are reliably paid on time and in full? So they always have cash on hand for maintenance fees, or are there some handshake agreements that can accumulate interest? They're a form of organised crime, so how many palms are being greased? How are all of these things affected if there's a sudden extra expense, such as major damage to one or more ships? If there income is entirely dependent on their piracy, then keeping on top of their expenses requires constant raiding; the accumulated value of their treasure being lower than the cost of all their maintenance doesn't mean that it's never going to run out. So they have anybody to advise them to try investing in a more sustainable income stream? Do they have the standing to make such a thing feasible?<br /> <br /> What is the actual notice of their piracy, anyway? What have they been trying to accomplish with acquiring all of this treasure of they don't actually have a need to spend it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T04:51:17-07:00","id":1332268,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332250#post1332250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This Edition had kind of moved away from that model of &quot;having Resources allows you to make basically unlimited purchases of things with a level below your rating&quot; in favour of a greater focus on limited quantities at any given time, and handling paying of debts.<br /> <br /> Is the wealth of these characters in a form liquid enough to ensure that their crews are reliably paid on time and in full? So they always have cash on hand for maintenance fees, or are there some handshake agreements that can accumulate interest? They're a form of organised crime, so how many palms are being greased? How are all of these things affected if there's a sudden extra expense, such as major damage to one or more ships? If there income is entirely dependent on their piracy, then keeping on top of their expenses requires constant raiding; the accumulated value of their treasure being lower than the cost of all their maintenance doesn't mean that it's never going to run out. So they have anybody to advise them to try investing in a more sustainable income stream? Do they have the standing to make such a thing feasible?<br /> <br /> What is the actual notice of their piracy, anyway? What have they been trying to accomplish with acquiring all of this treasure of they don't actually have a need to spend it?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Actually I'm curious, I have no idea how to manage the Resources of my players. Did you find any guidelines in the books around the theme of your first sentence, focus on limited quantities and paying of debts ?<br /> <br /> Since my first plays, I really didn't have any good opportunities to do cool stuff with Ressources.<br /> <br /> Also, are people from your Command merit loyal to you, and they don't need to be paid ? I don't mean let them live in the dirt and die in sadness, but like I don't think I will ask one of my players to pay Ressources 3 to afford the loyalty an well being of the hundreds of men of the Command 3 merit, because I think it is already included in the cost of the merit. Or should I ?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T05:05:26-07:00","id":1332269,"title":"","content":"The concept of wealth can be hard for the characters themselves to handle . Like my character in wizards lunars game is a maugli / Tarzan like character who was raised in a jungle by a talking Bear and became the king of the jungle conquering the local man eating white apes. So his territory and influence is over ruins of a lost first age city and manse he has manse 5 and resources 5. But that\u2019s actual butt load of treasure the character himself had no concept of wealth and at best kept a pouch of rubies for playing marbles . So how does it work when you have fabulous wealth in treasure but no income ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12796-beast-of-bitter-oblivion","name":"Beast of Bitter Oblivion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12796_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T05:20:30-07:00","id":1332270,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332268#post1332268\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, are people from your Command merit loyal to you, and they don't need to be paid ? I don't mean let them live in the dirt and die in sadness, but like I don't think I will ask one of my players to pay Ressources 3 to afford the loyalty an well being of the hundreds of men of the Command 3 merit, because I think it is already included in the cost of the merit. Or should I ?</div> </div> </div> </div>You should.<br /> <br /> Command means they're your soldiers. Their job is working for you. Who's paying them if not you? How are they eating and being equipped?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beast of Bitter Oblivion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332269#post1332269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The concept of wealth can be hard for the characters themselves to handle . Like my character in wizards lunars game is a maugli / Tarzan like character who was raised in a jungle by a talking Bear and became the king of the jungle conquering the local man eating white apes. So his territory and influence is over ruins of a lost first age city and manse he has manse 5 and resources 5. But that\u2019s actual butt load of treasure the character himself had no concept of wealth and at best kept a pouch of rubies for playing marbles . So how does it work when you have fabulous wealth in treasure but no income ?</div> </div> </div> </div>The book specifically describes Resources as &quot;income&quot; and the economic level you live at.<br /> <br /> If you live in the dirt in the jungle, I'd peg that as Resources 0.<br /> <br /> The Merit is about how easy it is for you to acquire things: whether on credit, with silver or with pretty sea shells. Having a bag of shiny marbles which you don't realise are valuable doesn't help you charter a merchant navy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T08:02:17-07:00","id":1332285,"title":"","content":"Your Command dots will work for you if you have Resources 0, but they'll probably have to rely a lot more on pillaging than they would if you could pay them.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T08:19:47-07:00","id":1332287,"title":"","content":"We're basically rebels raiding our nation because we were enslaved","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T08:53:57-07:00","id":1332296,"title":"","content":"Alucard's probably right then; presumably your soldiers aren't motivated by pay, but by anger and defence. But they do need to eat, etc. So pillaging and looting is going to be the order of the day.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T10:28:11-07:00","id":1332323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332296#post1332296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Alucard's probably right then; presumably your soldiers aren't motivated by pay, but by anger and defence. But they do need to eat, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, now I want to look into things around how the finances of the IRA worked during the War of Independence. After all, a lot of those lads were going of into the wilderness so they couldn't exactly have a normal source of income, and it wasn't exactly a salaried group, but people have more than just basic needs of food, clothing and fuel (and in their case guns and bullets). Was Collins sending each cell commander a budget, and they'd distribute it among the soldiers as a stipend when they'd go into town for the necessities or entertainments?<br /> <br /> Hmm, have to look into a good source...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T10:28:25-07:00","id":1332324,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332270#post1332270\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You should.<br /> <br /> Command means they're your soldiers. Their job is working for you. Who's paying them if not you? How are they eating and being equipped?<br /> <br /> <br /> The book specifically describes Resources as &amp;quot;income&amp;quot; and the economic level you live at.<br /> <br /> If you live in the dirt in the jungle, I'd peg that as Resources 0.<br /> <br /> The Merit is about how easy it is for you to acquire things: whether on credit, with silver or with pretty sea shells. Having a bag of shiny marbles which you don't realise are valuable doesn't help you charter a merchant navy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well I live in ruins of a palace manse in the ruins of lost first age city or outpost that\u2019s been overrun by jungle over millennia . So what your saying even tho I have the scarlet empresses ransom worth of orichalcum dooorknobs I still have resources 0. ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12796-beast-of-bitter-oblivion","name":"Beast of Bitter Oblivion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12796_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T10:38:23-07:00","id":1332325,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332268#post1332268\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, are people from your Command merit loyal to you, and they don't need to be paid ? I don't mean let them live in the dirt and die in sadness, but like I don't think I will ask one of my players to pay Ressources 3 to afford the loyalty an well being of the hundreds of men of the Command 3 merit, because I think it is already included in the cost of the merit. Or should I ?</div> </div> </div> </div>I might be able to answer the rest of your questions later, but for the moment, Command is a Merit that I would read as accepting a responsibility in addition to receiving an entitlement.<br /> <br /> One can define their particular group as fanatical personal devotees or people dedicated to a higher cause such that they are truly committed to living with the greatest austerity, I think the game's narrative should be concerned with keeping an eye on them; making sure that any high tension and extremism don't lead to unwanted atrocities.<br /> <br /> The Merits are separate, but Command without Resources should either be for circumstances where somebody else is footing the bill or where one across the plot complications of financing their army.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T10:41:31-07:00","id":1332327,"title":"","content":"Like for Dragon blooded just command is probably house troops while command supported by resources could be your personal command.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12796-beast-of-bitter-oblivion","name":"Beast of Bitter Oblivion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12796_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T10:50:41-07:00","id":1332329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332323#post1332323\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hmm, now I want to look into things around how the finances of the IRA worked during the War of Independence. After all, a lot of those lads were going of into the wilderness so they couldn't exactly have a normal source of income, and it wasn't exactly a salaried group, but people have more than just basic needs of food, clothing and fuel (and in their case guns and bullets). Was Collins sending each cell commander a budget, and they'd distribute it among the soldiers as a stipend when they'd go into town for the necessities or entertainments?<br /> <br /> Hmm, have to look into a good source...</div> </div> </div> </div>Tell me if you find something, I'd be interested.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T15:30:24-07:00","id":1332377,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332329#post1332329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Tell me if you find something, I'd be interested.</div> </div> </div> </div>Having looked into it a bit on Wikipedia...<br /> <br /> It does actually seem as though being part of a flying column hiding in the deep countryside for ambushes really was something where you were living in a good deal of austerity. The rebels did <i>have</i> money, but the degree of secrecy they needed to operate under seems to have been an obstacle under the best of circumstances, and I'm guessing that any time a flying column needed to purchase supplies they needed to rely on the goodwill of local merchants that they'd be paid back by the rebel government at the earliest convenience. It probably helped that the general political will of the populace was on the side of the Dail and the IRA; the earliest stages of Irish independent government were fairly dependent on people at large kind of playing along with its attempts at bureaucracy when offices were in constant danger of needing to pick up and run to a new building, leaving most of their paperwork behind or burning it.<br /> <br /> I'd forgotten that the matter of how they paid for guns has the straightforward answer of &quot;they kind of didn't&quot;; at the best of times Ireland was not exactly a place where the British would have been happy with a large trade in private firearms, so the only viable domestic source was raiding them from the police or the army. Attempts were made to purchase them from abroad, but those would run into the alternating difficulties in getting secret money to the arms dealers and then getting the goods back to the soldiers.<br /> <br /> Turns out, it's kind of difficult to run weapons in when you have no land borders and your enemy has the most powerful navy in the world.<br /> <br /> Still, the First Dail did raise a decent amount of money, I suppose that having a limited ability to spend it on anything before the British negotiated a settlement have them a bit more of a financial leg to stand on as a legitimate government.<br /> <br /> As it goes in a scenario for Exalted, I can see some parts being harder; a rebellion in hiding probably can't effectively carry its cash around with it, so you might not need only Resources, but to establish in play a network that connects them with the people that you'll want to pay for supplies and good will. There might not exactly be a nationalism to hold things up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T15:49:57-07:00","id":1332381,"title":"","content":"True, but on the other hand if your oppressors are armed with spears, you don't need so much money or manufacturing capacity to compete with them. A lot of rebel peasants may already have weapons theh normally use for farming (which may be where the IRA got a lot of their weapons, no idea; I know the Orangemen had a ton of guns for hunting and farming, which is why they were able to threaten to rebel if London gave Ulster to the new Irish state. But then, just because Orangemen had plenty of access to guns doesn't mean Catholics did. I don't know if they were still applying the Bill of Rights).<br /> <br /> Good point about nationalism. It's more likely to be about oppressive taxes or religious suppression. But religious identity can supply much of the same impetus, even if the end goal is different.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T16:32:38-07:00","id":1332383,"title":"","content":"I don't want to take this thread on a whole different direction, so as a final point that remains tangentially connected to the resource needs of a rebellious group I'll just say that I think that historically, religious oppression has not quite carried that same capacity for a civilian population that can shelter the armed rebels, because I think that it more frequently takes a form where like, everyone from the heterodox group is being persecuted simultaneously. I think 19th to 20th century nationalism often operated against and took advantage of an authority that tried to promote a party line of attempting to accommodate and integrate the group that the rebels are hiding among.<br /> <br /> That said, the other side to it is when there are less centralised administrations with pervasive governing apparatus and smaller armies rebellion from a position of secrecy is less necessary. You just kind of raise an army in a region where the government is weak. Your roving band is a bit less of a mobile cell in a decentralised network than it is a group of bandits with a certain political bent.<br /> <br /> Mind, historically such groups have sometimes managed to form the seed around which a wider rebellious army crystallizes, like in say the start of Han China. Of course, in cases like that your resource needs increase dramatically.<br /> <br /> But to bring it back to the subject of portraying that roving bandit group, I feel as though the big thing is that the matter of needing to address shortages of basic necessities should be something that comes up periodically, and with a measure of escalation. Like, every now and then a Story includes a scene where the players need to engage with some merchant who can hook them up with what they need, and those scenes have variations in the characters being interacted with each time or its the same contact but things keep getting harder for them and costs keep going up. One might introduce a chapter or even a whole Story in which something bigger than just haggling with the merchant is needed to secure something important or necessary, out to safeguard a supply chain that is under threat. And it's up to the players to look at these circumstances and challenges and realise that they ought to proactively engage in a more long term solution.<br /> <br /> Those are things related to the Resources system, but not exactly the Merit, which I think is more about representing somebody who both had more of a reliable stream of income and circumstances that dictate something to continuously spend it on.<br /> <br /> That would be why I wouldn't think the guy in the jungle sitting on a trove of previous metal really qualifies as having a Resources Merit. Not before they get to a point where they're engaging with people that will exchange that stuff for something the character finds useful or desirable. These things aren't objective measures of quantity and value, they're utilities for gameplay and narrative.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T18:14:45-07:00","id":1332403,"title":"","content":"Resources has often been a bit tricky to try to fit in with other statted Merits, for us.<br /> <br /> On the one hand, it's got dots. On the other hand, if you can sell your doorknobs to buy a nation, why keep track of it?<br /> <br /> For this edition, we've been making Resources (and also things like Command, Allies, etc) things that you can build up with a leadership task. Something like: each dot requires (dot^2) successes, with a threshold of 5, and we can make a stunt and roll for it once per session while we're waiting on other people to show up. Or waiting on take-out to arrive. <br /> <br /> If you don't have the dots, you can still pay for things (or order people around) but always with some sort of hitch or inconvenience. The market for doorknobs is a bit niche and needs just the right introductions.<br /> <br /> I'm fully expecting Resources to break down as a game system when we hit Essence 3 or 4. At that point, we'll disregard it or simply describe the army of bureaucrats whisking funding requests directly to the treasury.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1188890-momentum-exalted\" target=\"_blank\">Momentum Exalted!</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6192-simon-darkstep","name":"Simon Darkstep","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T19:39:15-07:00","id":1332413,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Simon Darkstep</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332403#post1332403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the one hand, it's got dots. On the other hand, if you can sell your doorknobs to buy a nation, why keep track of it?</div> </div> </div> </div>...<i>Can</i> you?<br /> <br /> You say it has dots, but I don't understand what significance you attach to that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Simon Darkstep</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">For this edition, we've been making Resources (and also things like Command, Allies, etc) things that you can build up with a leadership task. Something like: each dot requires (dot^2) successes, with a threshold of 5, and we can make a stunt and roll for it once per session while we're waiting on other people to show up. Or waiting on take-out to arrive.</div> </div> </div> </div>You don't really seem very enthusiastic about it when you're talking about sitting it into an empty space like that. Why do you feel obligated to make increasing the value in it such perfunctory busywork? Do you not want to engage in the narratives surrounding the increase of wealth, property and social standing?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Simon Darkstep</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm fully expecting Resources to break down as a game system when we hit Essence 3 or 4. At that point, we'll disregard it or simply describe the army of bureaucrats whisking funding requests directly to the treasury.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your Essence level is directly tied to how much authority the characters possess?<br /> <br /> The point about official funding did bring up the point that the idea of government funds as a thing distinct from the personal fortunes of the monarch or oligarchs is a relatively recent phenomenon in most parts of the world, and is dependent on things like a robust economy and widespread centralised authority, so in a lot of cases Resources 4 or 5 is going to be kind of necessary for being in charge of a country. Either that or the preceding story ought to cover the events that have led to establishing that kind of government in parts of the world outside of the Realm.<br /> <br /> Mind, it's a story Merit with a primary narrative purpose anyway, so the question of how it's increased and applied are already mostly focused on narrative. In that case, personal Resources 5 and being in command of a government with equivalent budgets seems a bit indistinguishable in terms of practical gameplay.<br /> <br /> Mind, I get the idea of not personally wanting a story to deal with that side of it and letting it mostly be handwave with an idea that somebody trustworthy is handling the finances offstage (though it does make those downtime dice rolls seem even more pointless), but it doesn't exactly am like helpful advice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T23:17:45-07:00","id":1332422,"title":"","content":"So I talked to my players and told them to think about what to buy and use their treasure on. The Captain is going to try to build up an Armada and payoff some spy masters to recruit more Lost Eggs he's taken to the idea of having them because he rescued a renegade DB- a Wood Archer (who is absolutely -not- Naked Snake I swear). The Exorcist is looking at Hearthstones for him or his Familiar. He gets overwhelmed by all the options easy and forgets about his powers so he's looking/thinking of fun RP abilities more than anything mechanic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Please be warned: this is not champagne, this is most likely a duck.<br /> -<i>Chausse</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#800080\"><i>Message me for Japanese translations.</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8830-sorcerous-overlord","name":"Sorcerous Overlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-24T01:49:29-07:00","id":1332427,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332383#post1332383\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That would be why I wouldn't think the guy in the jungle sitting on a trove of previous metal really qualifies as having a Resources Merit. Not before they get to a point where they're engaging with people that will exchange that stuff for something the character finds useful or desirable. These things aren't objective measures of quantity and value, they're utilities for gameplay and narrative.</div> </div> </div> </div>Beast's point when he raised his character's vast treasure trove wasn't that he can't spend it; he has no understanding of wealth, but his friends do, and regularly use one of his gems to, say, ransom some slaves. His point was he has a load of wealth but no income; the gems don't make more money. Once he's spent them, he's spent them.<br /> <br /> Our old ST used to sometimes say, if we found a big load of treasure, &quot;you can make one Resources 4 purchase&quot; or something like that. That works okay.<br /> But in practice, I don't think it matters too much, if the PCs invest it somehow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-24T02:42:40-07:00","id":1332431,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332427#post1332427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Beast's point when he raised his character's vast treasure trove wasn't that he can't spend it; he has no understanding of wealth, but his friends do, and regularly use one of his gems to, say, ransom some slaves. His point was he has a load of wealth but no income; the gems don't make more money. Once he's spent them, he's spent them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough, but the post still said that the character had Resources 5, and either way I'm suggesting that the character does not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-24T10:44:26-07:00","id":1332448,"title":"","content":"You may find it fruitful to have a conversation about how the players feel about sacrificing their backgrounds for certain things. Resources is one of those backgrounds that you can reasonably expect to fluctuate; would it be okay if a character attacked their resources? Or if the players exchanged resources for new connections?<br /> <br /> Edit: It looks like that's what y'all discussed when looking for new story ideas. For me, I really like things that can't be gained through dice rolls. The story of The Count of Monte Cristo, for example, has several twists and turns about a character with effective Resources N/A. The same is true for a lot of Culture stories by Iain Banks. It's sort of like the Superman conundrum: what do you do when something that's critical for most people is no longer a consideration for your character?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5706,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5706-zeal","user":"Zeal","date":". 08-24-2019, 01:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5706-zeal","name":"Zeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5706_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-24T14:05:51-07:00","id":1332470,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332413#post1332413\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> ...<i>Can</i> you?<br /> <br /> You say it has dots, but I don't understand what significance you attach to that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair question!<br /> <br /> If something has dots or stats associated with it, then it seems that it's meant to be tracked, and that it has an associated XP cost or story element to go with it.<br /> <br /> At some point, though, Resources and some other Merits seem to behave more like stuntable background elements. If a character has conquered lands, Manses, and so forth, why could their player not simply describe in the exchange of some of that wealth, without having a separate stat for it? <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You don't really seem very enthusiastic about it when you're talking about sitting it into an empty space like that. Why do you feel obligated to make increasing the value in it such perfunctory busywork? Do you not want to engage in the narratives surrounding the increase of wealth, property and social standing? </div> </div> </div>After the introductory stage of an Exalted Chronicle, I'm <i>not </i>very enthusiastic about it. In our games so far, it's been much more the case that stacks of money exist from our other conquests, and we either wind up adjusting Resources by fiat - describing what the characters have gained by other means - or disregarding it. <br /> <br /> It is a pretty common trope for us, though, that our brand new Chosen tend to have empty pockets. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The busywork question is astute though! There's a bit of background on that, relevant to our current Chronicle, in fact. <br /> <br /> Firstly, one of my players wanted to do a Craft-supernal Twilight. We wanted progress on crafting Artifacts and other big projects to be meaningful, but not sidetrack a game session for rolls and not to be completely trivialized away.<br /> <br /> Also, my friends and I have a non-game-night movie night. Not everybody can make it, and it's after work so we're usually pretty wiped out for mathy things.<br /> <br /> So, we put those together ... on our non-game nights we can do a set of rolls for crafting, before we get movies going. It keeps pace roughly with the ongoing story and we know about how well projects are coming along.<br /> <br /> That actually worked out so well that we borrowed the leadership rules and made use of the same off-game time to progress on other things, without using up precious game session time. <br /> <br /> At some point, I'm expecting things like Resources will become uninteresting again, but we'll see how it goes. It's working pretty well for now!<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your Essence level is directly tied to how much authority the characters possess?<br /> <br /> The point about official funding did bring up the point that the idea of government funds as a thing distinct from the personal fortunes of the monarch or oligarchs is a relatively recent phenomenon in most parts of the world, and is dependent on things like a robust economy and widespread centralised authority, so in a lot of cases Resources 4 or 5 is going to be kind of necessary for being in charge of a country. Either that or the preceding story ought to cover the events that have led to establishing that kind of government in parts of the world outside of the Realm.<br /> <br /> Mind, it's a story Merit with a primary narrative purpose anyway, so the question of how it's increased and applied are already mostly focused on narrative. In that case, personal Resources 5 and being in command of a government with equivalent budgets seems a bit indistinguishable in terms of practical gameplay.<br /> <br /> Mind, I get the idea of not personally wanting a story to deal with that side of it and letting it mostly be handwave with an idea that somebody trustworthy is handling the finances offstage (though it does make those downtime dice rolls seem even more pointless), but it doesn't exactly am like helpful advice. </div> </div> </div><br /> It's not directly tied, no. <br /> <br /> But our Chronicles tend to reach a point where being budget-constrained doesn't seem to fit in the story. It's an old concern, and if it shows up in the story after a certain point, feels like a reminiscence about where we started off. <br /> <br /> This isn't specific to Exalted, either. We could just as easily have a group of 10th-level D20 characters, planning their next great quest. They might look back and remember when they barely had two coppers to rub together and had to go forage a meal in the forest, up until their first haul of loot from the goblin camp. But nobody in that group is going to be short of money or supplies, unless it's become a specific story element to make them so.<br /> <br /> Essence levels feel like a level of overall progress to me. At E1, I pretty much imagine the new Chosen are getting the feel of their new power, trying to figure out the world around them, and survive - including scrapping for funds. By E2, we've got a pretty good grasp of the world and probably know what the story is starting to look like. By E3, we're movers and shakers, and more of the world and NPCs start to revolve around the Circle, instead of the other way around. By E4, we're probably the masters of dominions and destiny. By E5, we're the masters of all we survey.<br /> <br /> That might just be how it works in my head, though!<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1188890-momentum-exalted\" target=\"_blank\">Momentum Exalted!</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6192-simon-darkstep","name":"Simon Darkstep","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-24T14:27:22-07:00","id":1332471,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beast of Bitter Oblivion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332324#post1332324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well I live in ruins of a palace manse in the ruins of lost first age city or outpost that\u2019s been overrun by jungle over millennia . So what your saying even tho I have the scarlet empresses ransom worth of orichalcum dooorknobs I still have resources 0. ?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's your character. You tell me.<br /> <br /> If my character lived in a ruin and didn't buy anything because they genuinely didn't understand money, credit and income then I'd play that as Resources 0. Those doorknobs aren't doing anything -- it's just fluff. If you later learn about money and want to raise your Resources rating, then there's your justification for spending XP on a Story merit.<br /> <br /> If my character &quot;doesn't understand&quot; money as a charming quirk and ends up handing over diamonds the size of a child's fist to settle a 2 koku debt, then I'd play that as Resources 5. Your &quot;not understanding&quot; isn't doing anything -- it's just fluff.<br /> <br /> Somewhere in the middle? Take Resources 5 and a Flaw. The Flaw won't have any effect if it nevers comes up so it's not unbalanced, and you can have the dramatic effect when it does.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332427#post1332427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Beast's point when he raised his character's vast treasure trove wasn't that he can't spend it; he has no understanding of wealth, but his friends do, and regularly use one of his gems to, say, ransom some slaves. His point was he has a load of wealth but no income; the gems don't make more money. Once he's spent them, he's spent them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well it's a Story Merit. If you're spending but not earning it should go down. *shrug*<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Simon Darkstep</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332470#post1332470\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not directly tied, no. <br /> <br /> But our Chronicles tend to reach a point where being budget-constrained doesn't seem to fit in the story. It's an old concern, and if it shows up in the story after a certain point, feels like a reminiscence about where we started off. <br /> <br /> This isn't specific to Exalted, either. We could just as easily have a group of 10th-level D20 characters, planning their next great quest. They might look back and remember when they barely had two coppers to rub together and had to go forage a meal in the forest, up until their first haul of loot from the goblin camp. But nobody in that group is going to be short of money or supplies, unless it's become a specific story element to make them so.<br /> <br /> Essence levels feel like a level of overall progress to me. At E1, I pretty much imagine the new Chosen are getting the feel of their new power, trying to figure out the world around them, and survive - including scrapping for funds. By E2, we've got a pretty good grasp of the world and probably know what the story is starting to look like. By E3, we're movers and shakers, and more of the world and NPCs start to revolve around the Circle, instead of the other way around. By E4, we're probably the masters of dominions and destiny. By E5, we're the masters of all we survey.<br /> <br /> That might just be how it works in my head, though!</div> </div> </div> </div>Resources is a Story Merit and nothing about being able to hit things really hard with a sword or being able to summon a cloud to ride on directly translate to being rich.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying the Exalted have a hard time getting rich, but you should have expenses. Stripping the dead and selling their stuff shouldn't be as easy as in a video game, and certainly shouldn't be the basis for the Resources Merit. Resources is described as income and it plays into the plot. At E3 you've got a neat statline and some past adventures under your belt but that doesn't directly translate to goblin hoard. You need to acquire income to have income, and your expenses increase with your holdings.<br /> <br /> A Dynast is Resources 3. What are you doing to compete economically with a prince in the most powerful nation in Creation and why does the Wyld Hunt not care?<br /> <br /> A starting Solar can easily go from Resources 0 to Resources 5 in a single session. It's not hard. But it shouldn't &quot;just happen&quot;. You need to start a business or collect taxes or something.<br /> <br /> Or not. Resistance, Craft and Survival Charms under your belt, Daiklave on your back, thirst for justice in your heart -- who needs money?<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=HqcbgSpHMFs\" target=\"_blank\">OPs complaint about PCs having nothing they want to spend their dragon hoard on is very in genre.</a><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Simon Darkstep</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332470#post1332470\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At some point, though, Resources and some other Merits seem to behave more like stuntable background elements. If a character has conquered lands, Manses, and so forth, why could their player not simply describe in the exchange of some of that wealth, without having a separate stat for it? </div> </div> </div> </div>That's pretty much exactly what a Story Merit is. If you are stripping the wealth from conquered lands then you keep track on that wealth and its impact through the Resources Merit. If you don't strip the land and instead invest in your holdings then take something like Contacts or Influence instead.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 08-24-2019, 06:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T01:17:34-07:00","id":1332508,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332431#post1332431\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Fair enough, but the post still said that the character had Resources 5, and either way I'm suggesting that the character does not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because it's non-sustainable?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T01:44:23-07:00","id":1332512,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332471#post1332471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If my character &quot;doesn't understand&quot; money as a charming quirk and ends up handing over diamonds the size of a child's fist to settle a 2 koku debt, then I'd play that as Resources 5. Your &quot;not understanding&quot; isn't doing anything -- it's just fluff.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is pretty much how it works. He plays with shiny rocks, the other PCs scam him a bit (but not much actually), and also use his shiny gems to buy stuff the party needs. I've found it quite useful actually, as ST; the game is in the far, far, north-east, a pretty poor area, so I don't want the players to feel rich. On the other hand, I really get bored with games where the PCs have to trade, count up their income and outgoings (that's why I didn't like Traveller when I played it), and so on. I want to skip past that. There's slaves to rescue, plots to untangle, mercenary mutants to fight, etc, there's no time for accounting.<br /> <br /> I just feel for the sake of versimillitude, I can't just go &quot;yeah, you guys find a bunch of food and supplies for some reason, carry on&quot;. So having the one guy who carries jewels in the party is really useful as they can use it to skip past those basic problems, he just gives a shiny rock to whichever PCs missed the session and they go away to a market town, and come back with supplies for a couple of sessions. But on the other hand, he's far too dumb to do anything very disruptive with his money, like cause massive inflation or hire an army of mercenaries. And if later on they do actually need a whole bunch of cash (to supply a starving kingdom or something), the other PCs will be able to explain to him how to use it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Stripping the dead and selling their stuff shouldn't be as easy as in a video game, and certainly shouldn't be the basis for the Resources Merit. </div> </div> </div>I find the way artefacts and cash work in Exalted quite interesing, vis-a-vis this and how it's different from computer games/DnD.<br /> <br /> Demons, monsters, animals, etc, don't have much saleable stuff. Soldiers have armour and weapons, but without knights in full plate, it's probably not going to get you a vast amount of money. I mean, armour and swords are valuable, sure, but you're not going to get rich from it unless you're some kind of psycho slaughter monster who ambushes entire units. Where are you going to sell all this, for one thing?<br /> And then there's stripping Dragonblood corpses. You've now got some unsellable artefacts which their families will want back. You can attune them yourself, but chances are they're no better (probably worse) than any artefact weapons or armour you already had (which could be orichalcum, plus you may have evocations). And you can't really attune 3 different kind of weapons for the different benefits the way a Level 15 fighter might have a giantbane axe, flaming dagger, and holy silver hammer in his bag in case his +3 longsword isn't effective. That'd eat up all your motes.<br /> Whereas even just my level 5 Wizard has like 4 or 5 minor magic items in his pockets, a magic wand, a magic circlet, 2 spellbooks, etc.<br /> <br /> So high-essence Solar characters often have some spare jade weapons lying around, that either get reforged by the Crafter, or given to whatever retainers they have who can attune them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or not. Resistance, Craft and Survival Charms under your belt, Daiklave on your back, thirst for justice in your heart -- who needs money? </div> </div> </div>Most PCs I see barely bother much with money. Generally, there's one really rich character, (the Twilight crafter, the gorilla-Lunar with a bunch of jewels, the Night-caste Prince) and everyone lives off them (sometimes without really telling them...). Or they seize a country and live off the tax, of course.<br /> Like a medieval Lord and his retainers, I guess.<br /> <br /> Plus you often get a monk or feral Lunar or something who just has no interest in cash.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's pretty much exactly what a Story Merit is. If you are stripping the wealth from conquered lands then you keep track on that wealth and its impact through the Resources Merit. If you don't strip the land and instead invest in your holdings then take something like Contacts or Influence instead. </div> </div> </div>Eh, if you invest in holdings that then produce more money, I'd say that's Resources. I mean, you can't really just hit up Contacts for money constantly. (Could be Backing under some circumstances though.)<br /> <br /> Like, our old Twilight made a load of money selling amazing jewellery, and eventually bought a salt mine. I guess technically he's stripping the land (it is a mine), but it'll take decades, so for now he's turned a load of static wealth into income.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 08-25-2019, 04:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T03:46:16-07:00","id":1332517,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332512#post1332512\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Eh, if you invest in holdings that then produce more money, I'd say that's Resources. I mean, you can't really just hit up Contacts for money constantly. (Could be Backing under some circumstances though.)<br /> <br /> Like, our old Twilight made a load of money selling amazing jewellery, and eventually bought a salt mine. I guess technically he's stripping the land (it is a mine), but it'll take decades, so for now he's turned a load of static wealth into income.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's pretty much the exact default position of &quot;starting a business&quot;. Absolutely a-okay. Apologies if I was unclear.<br /> <br /> My point is that Story Merits are you leveraging the &quot;stuntable background elements&quot;. That's literally all they are and what they're supposed to be. They used to be called &quot;Backgrounds&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T04:59:16-07:00","id":1332522,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332508#post1332508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Because it's non-sustainable?</div> </div> </div> </div>JohnDoe244 has already basically covered every point I would make about it. I mildly disagree on some of the specifics, but they're not worth bringing up.<br /> <br /> One thing I often think about with Resources is a line that has been in the game from the beginning; that the stipend of a Dynast is something that should be capable of allowing them to live comfortably for the rest of their lives, but they don't, because they're surrounded by social expectations that would hold such frugality in contempt.<br /> <br /> I think that part of the fun and the challenge of playing supremely wealthy people in a setting such as this is the conspicuous consumption that tends to accumulate around them. Something that, like the car with Dynasts, is not always a matter of people indulging in the extreme, how there's often a component of people taking you widely being dependent on flaunting wealth.<br /> <br /> It makes me think about things like accounts of the pageantry of Medieval tournaments, or of Heian-kyo Japan, where the upper classes live surrounded by art and draped in luxury and swimming in fine, costly food and drink (when marzipan was something rate and difficult to make, it would be a status symbol to serve detailed sculptures of the stuff), exchanging valuable gifts as a matter of course, and surrounded not only by servants, but servants who are themselves dressed in fancy uniforms.<br /> <br /> This in addition to practical expenses. Do you have an army? Do you have infrastructure that you have a wish or a responsibility to maintain and expand?<br /> <br /> These are things that underlie the fact that historically, it's fairly uncommon to have people that are so rich that they're not constantly hovering on the edge of... not having cash reserves, which is different than being destitute, not least because rich people have an easier time getting loans or lines of credit.<br /> <br /> I don't think these things need to be tracked down to the smallest transaction, not least because even the lowest rating of Resources would seem to denote having a system that periodically returns money to you. The question of how you scheme to acquire more money should probably mostly come up under circumstances where a sudden unexpected expense comes up but oh no, you have no liquid assets on hand and you can't really pay for stuff in land (and shouldn't anyway, it's worth more in the long run), so you're forced to engage with something like a friend or a creditor or personally overseeing some transaction, or engaging in a contest or wager to get the money in hand.<br /> <br /> Either an unexpected expense or a scene that opens with your accountant informing you that your current expenses are exceeding your available assets or likely income.<br /> <br /> I think a thing that's often overlooked about land-owning aristocracy is how cash poor they tend to be, all the way up to the early 19th century in at least the case of England. I've got an interesting little book about the Chippendale furniture manufacturers in which a Yorkshire nobleman is quoted as saying something to the effect of &quot;I only get paid once a year, so my retailers should expect no different&quot;. Which, yeah, makes sense when the foundation of your wealth is rents from farmers who are only bringing in one or two crops a year. But you're still buying stuff all throughout the year, and it's expensive. Hope somebody taught you how to balance your cheque book.<br /> <br /> Right down to a conception of the practice of needing to prioritise which cheques you can afford to bounce this quarter, so it's more complicated than &quot;whoops, I spent more than I make, guess I'm ruined&quot;.<br /> <br /> None of these are strictly necessary, but I feel as though the main point of defining the level of finance and society that you're operating at by way of buying the Merit at character creation is that one finds the premise fun and interesting. If it isn't, then well... worry about acquiring money through some relatively straightforward means only when you need to buy something important.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T05:30:34-07:00","id":1332527,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332512#post1332512\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is pretty much how it works. He plays with shiny rocks, the other PCs scam him a bit (but not much actually), and also use his shiny gems to buy stuff the party needs. I've found it quite useful actually, as ST; the game is in the far, far, north-east, a pretty poor area, so I don't want the players to feel rich. On the other hand, I really get bored with games where the PCs have to trade, count up their income and outgoings (that's why I didn't like Traveller when I played it), and so on. I want to skip past that. There's slaves to rescue, plots to untangle, mercenary mutants to fight, etc, there's no time for accounting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, if no player wants to use Bureaucracy to have a direct handle on their accounting (which I think really shouldn't consist of needing to keep track of the actual numbers any more than rolling Medicine to diagnose needs anything like realistic or detailed medical terminology) then it's fine for there to be a non-player character who mediates that primarily for the purpose of laying down plot hooks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">So having the one guy who carries jewels in the party is really useful as they can use it to skip past those basic problems, he just gives a shiny rock to whichever PCs missed the session and they go away to a market town, and come back with supplies for a couple of sessions.</div> </div> </div> </div>Heh, this makes me think of that one It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia episode revolving around a pyramid scheme and time share, in which a couple of the guys are trying to sell off some of their time share and the not all there guy who takes them up on it offers to pay them in berries. And the guys are like &quot;no, pay us in money, not a product that we then need to go out and sell to others&quot;.<br /> <br /> Or hell, if there's one thing that I'll give to the otherwise inexecrable Eragon book series, it's how at the start the dumbass kid tries to buy meat with a giant stone, and the guy refuses to take it because he has no frame of reference for how valuable it is and can't really break it down for smaller transactions anyway.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying that's what you're thinking of exactly, but it sounds enough like it and does remind me of how many properties do take it at face value that somebody can just be handed a chunk of precious commodity as a form of payment.<br /> <br /> But on the other hand, he's far too dumb to do anything very disruptive with his money, like cause massive inflation or hire an army of mercenaries. And if later on they do actually need a whole bunch of cash (to supply a starving kingdom or something), the other PCs will be able to explain to him how to use it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Demons, monsters, animals, etc, don't have much saleable stuff. Soldiers have armour and weapons, but without knights in full plate, it's probably not going to get you a vast amount of money. I mean, armour and swords are valuable, sure, but you're not going to get rich from it unless you're some kind of psycho slaughter monster who ambushes entire units. Where are you going to sell all this, for one thing?</div> </div> </div> </div>Second-hand merchant.<br /> <br /> It's funny how in our modern throw-away cultures we've kind of forgotten how most of human history has been dependent on things being constantly reused, either through gifting or through a thriving commercial sector that deals in that stuff, to a point where it often fails to make it into our depictions of the past or something resembling it.<br /> <br /> If one is out on the road, it might be worth the Storyteller introducing a minor caravan or something containing people who might be willing to take the excess off of your hands for convenience purposes. I don't think any of the resulting cash in hand needs to be tracked down to the penny, people just keep in mind a baseline of what conveniences it can secure them the next time they stop over somewhere. Bureaucracy actions and Charms might help to stretch those out a bit in terms of bargaining or finding the best deals. You don't need to account for everything, just the Storyteller saying that you've got enough in the wallet to do, like, three useful things, which might be stretched to four or five if one plays their cards right.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And then there's stripping Dragonblood corpses. You've now got some unsellable artefacts which their families will want back.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ransom them. Or just sell them to a Guild member who can.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or they seize a country and live off the tax, of course.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because historically nothing has been as simple or as straightforward as extracting taxes. You just declare yourself supreme executive power, and the populace will be tripping over themselves to give you honest accountings of their holdings, and down payments. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Incidentally, the Domesday Book was William of Normandy's decreed compilation of all of England's truly fascinating and beautiful domes, and the English Civil War was fought over what style of hat people should wear (the victors were, of course, the ones who favoured the dome-shaped hats).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like a medieval Lord and his retainers, I guess.</div> </div> </div> </div>Retainers being one of the biggest, often ruinous expense for an aristocrat.<br /> <br /> I take it that the other player characters are eminently reasonable and in no way take somebody else being a personal ATM for granted?<br /> <br /> (I'm not saying that anybody strictly needs to, just that it's a thing worth thinking about)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Plus you often get a monk or feral Lunar or something who just has no interest in cash.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wandering mendicant style monks are <i>extremely</i> interested in cash, because they still need it to live off of, and it's a more convenient and reasonable to expect donation than people carrying around bags of food and fuel that they'll offer you a portion of.<br /> <br /> Monks who live in established monasteries care about cash in almost exactly the same way that aristocrats do. They'll spend it slightly differently, but they're still often quite rich on an institutional level, and even if sincerely spiritual, well that just motivates them towards spending their income on things of value to the people, good works and edifying monuments.<br /> <br /> Sure there are monks who live in much more isolated and impoverished conditions, but I'm not sure they make good player characters.<br /> <br /> I don't intend any of this to mean that people are playing games wrong, I just think that they're interesting things to engage with if one has a mind to.<br /> <br /> That and occasionally snark a bit, because they're fun things to write.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T08:38:29-07:00","id":1332540,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't intend any of this to mean that people are playing games wrong, I just think that they're interesting things to engage with if one has a mind to.<br /> <br /> That and occasionally snark a bit, because they're fun things to write.</div> </div> </div> </div>No problem; As far as I'm concerned, this is kind of the point of the forums; to engage with various stuff to do with Exalted, study it, compare it, think it through, and thus come up with inspiration or tips for running games, in terms of what works well, not well, or well for some things and not for others. And to give me something to do when I'm bored, obviously.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, if no player wants to use Bureaucracy to have a direct handle on their accounting (which I think really shouldn't consist of needing to keep track of the actual numbers any more than rolling Medicine to diagnose needs anything like realistic or detailed medical terminology) then it's fine for there to be a non-player character who mediates that primarily for the purpose of laying down plot hooks. </div> </div> </div>I didn't really mean about the exact numbers, though I can see why it looked like that, talking about other games. The whole point of Exalted using the Resources system is so you don't have to do that. I meant more stuff like the PCs having to constantly run around checking on their sources of income, buy supplies, and so on.<br /> My PCs have been traveling on a boat between towns (Vikings-meets-Camelot, a ruined half-sunken city of beastmen merchants, some abandoned fishing villages, a Raksha citadel surrounded by a den of thieves, and a small town of mutants). They've had animals, ex-slaves, crew, some Minotaurs, etc, on their ship, and the weather has been bad. So they need a lot of food, replacement supplies for the ship, fresh water, etc, etc. I can't really be bothered to go through how they acquire it all, because the plot is about rescuing some villagers who were kidnapped by mercenaries who framed Raksha, not survival, but I feel for the sake of verisimilitude it shouldn't just fall into their hands. So it's pretty useful to just go &quot;yeah, you buy it all from merchants with shiny jewels, now let's move on.&quot;<br /> <br /> I find that there's two kinds of rich characters; one kind doesn't really care about having loads of money, because they don't really care about buying a ton of stuff, they just want to not have to worry about money. So they don't really need an accountant who comes to them and says &quot;profits in your copper mine are down 4% due to pirate attacks, maybe you should go fight pirates for one story arc&quot;. They just want to throw money around. But they're also not doing much with their money, so there's no big issue here.<br /> Underfoot, Beasts' character, is one of those; primarily, Beast just thinks its funny to have a feral Lunar who has all these jewels and has no idea that they're worth so much. The Twilight in the previous game I ran was much like that as well; he didn't really care about money, he just didn't want to have to deal with money issues when he was busy in his secret underground puzzle-manse creating mutagenic plagues to improve the human condition.<br /> I guess the main plot point here you get is when there's a big disruption that means, say, they can't access their money or something, but I don't think that's something you'd want to do a lot, because this is a player who doesn't really find money-related plot that interesting.<br /> And there I think it's fine to just have an NPC who manages their money for them, but you don't really need one, because they're not going to run out of money any time soon anyway.<br /> <br /> <br /> (When I say &quot;money&quot;, this may not actually be cash. It might be food, supplies, clothes, etc. Owning 3 villages of serfs who supply you with food so you don't have to worry about where you get your food is much the same effect.)<br /> <br /> On the other hand, you have players who have all that Resources because they want to do things with it. Set up kingdoms, hire mercenary armies, centralise tax systems, build giant works of art, acquire stolen artefacts on the black market, bribe their enemies. This is a much more active approach, and should lead to a lot of plot. But of course, you may again have a player who's not that interested in the details; if the PCs don't want to deal with the gritty details, then having an NPC prime minister to run in and say &quot;Your majesty, the kingdom hasn't produced enough food for the winter, we need more&quot; or &quot;Your majesty, the neighbouring kingdom wants to invade to seize our treasury&quot;.=<br /> <br /> I suppose it's like Bureaucracy itself, as an ability (and charms). Some players take high Bureaucracy because they find it really interesting, and want to do a lot of deals and sort out the nitty-gritty stuff; others take it because they find it boring and want to be able to just roll dice until it goes away. Our Dawn caste general is an amazing administrator, but I don't think that's because the player finds army logistics interesting, I think he just didn't want to have to worry that much about it.<br /> <br /> Awkwardly, the player who runs a kingdom and loves developing it (opening mines, encouraging artisans to move there, using sorcery to improve the harvests) doesn't have any Bureaucracy charms, which when you're trying to turn a bunch mountain tribes and herders into a major military power with a massive agri-industrial base and a large capital city in a year or two, is a bit awkward.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not saying that's what you're thinking of exactly, but it sounds enough like it and does remind me of how many properties do take it at face value that somebody can just be handed a chunk of precious commodity as a form of payment.<br /> ...<br /> Second-hand merchant. </div> </div> </div>My point was... who buys 30 suits of lamellar armour, 30 short swords, 30 spears, 30 helmets and 30 shields? And how do you even get it to the shop? Because they're going to find it difficult to shift. Maybe if you can sell them to the local army, but presumably that's the army you just attacked.<br /> If you've got your own army, then pillaging is always popular, but for just, say, 5 PCs, looting the dead isn't going to be all that lucrative in terms of resale value (though they may be carrying cash at least). I mean you'll make some money, but not a ton of Resources.<br /> <br /> (I'm reminded of a DnD DM I had who told me how in one game he played there were villages where all the kids wore kobold leather armour as clothing, because the PCs had sold so much of it to the village merchant and he didn't know what else to do with it)<br /> <br /> But it is a good point about what money is to different people; the one place my Lunar PCs couldn't buy supplies with their jewels was a devastated village, because the slaves they'd brought back home and freed wouldn't have had any food if they sold it to the PCs for shiny useless rocks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> just the Storyteller saying that you've got enough in the wallet to do, like, three useful things, which might be stretched to four or five if one plays their cards right. </div> </div> </div>That's kind of what my old Dragonblood ST did when we looted ancient temples or whatever. &quot;You can buy two resources 3 purchases, or spread it further if you buy cheaper stuff&quot;. I think that's the most sensible thing to do with static cash, unless the players regularly loot like that, in which case you can just put it as normal Resources.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because historically nothing has been as simple or as straightforward as extracting taxes. You just declare yourself supreme executive power, and the populace will be tripping over themselves to give you honest accountings of their holdings, and down payments. </div> </div> </div>I just said live off the tax, not, say, hire an army. You don't need very effective tax collection to just live in your big house and not have to do manual labour (well, as long as you seize control of a place that already has a tax collection system, as the PCs generally do). So that's fine. The difficulty was with the warlord and queen who seized control of (two different countries of) farmers and nomads, then tried to build centralised states and have a standing army; both of them have found it extremely difficult (the queen has managed just about via massive amounts of sorcery to produce a ton of food, though this has led to different problems, but the warlord has killed a lot of revolting peasants, nobles, soldiers, priests, etc).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Retainers being one of the biggest, often ruinous expense for an aristocrat.<br /> <br /> I take it that the other player characters are eminently reasonable and in no way take somebody else being a personal ATM for granted? </div> </div> </div>So far, yes. Some because they're monks who've sworn vows of poverty, they just need food and somewhere to sleep from their patron, some because they're worried he'll be angry if they try to exploit him too much.<br /> I'm not going to claim that some of the PCs in the games I play aren't morally dubious, but none are really greedy for money. Some for power, territory, vengeance, sexy boys, etc, but not money.<br /> Hmmm... I guess the Night-Caste Prince's concubine (the Changing Moon) does spend a lot of money on clothes and jewels, but that's hardly unusual for a Prince's concubine.<br /> <br /> (That was also what I meant about the monks; one had an abbot who dealt with cash for him, but a Nephwrack ran off with his monastery. The other left his Immaculate Monastery due to doctrinal arguments. But the Prince let one stay in one of his many unused rooms for a while, and his servants just fed everyone there. Then after that a rich businesswoman let them stay at her teahouse and supplied them with the bread and water they live on. So they don't actually need any money. And before, when they were in monasteries, their Abbots dealt with the money so they didn't have to. And I guess the Monks in charge of accounts, of course.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T09:19:07-07:00","id":1332543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332522#post1332522\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> JohnDoe244 has already basically covered every point I would make about it. I mildly disagree on some of the specifics, but they're not worth bringing up.</div> </div> </div> </div>Does anyone else find this mildly unsettling?<br /> <br /> Like, half my posts on this forum are me disagreeing vehemently with Isator Levi. I feel like taking a contrary position just to protect my rep at this point.<br /> [Edit]<br /> Nothing personal. It's just an amusing observation that we normally disagree. I realise I might have been indelicate in expressing that. We've clearly and repeatedly expressed diametrically opposing visions of the game. (Istator's position is usually the one more consistent with the vision of the devs.) Agreeing on this and recent issues is <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=wlSYekcLuWM\" target=\"_blank\">ironic</a>.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332540#post1332540\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's kind of what my old Dragonblood ST did when we looted ancient temples or whatever. &quot;You can buy two resources 3 purchases, or spread it further if you buy cheaper stuff&quot;. I think that's the most sensible thing to do with static cash, unless the players regularly loot like that, in which case you can just put it as normal Resources.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah there we go.<br /> <br /> I hate this.<br /> <br /> 2E used it in a couple of printed modules but I really dislike it.<br /> <br /> The <i>point</i> of the Resources system is so you <i>don't have to</i> count the weight of every bent copper piece. &quot;Do I have two purchases left from the pirate hoard or one?&quot; is almost as bad.<br /> <br /> If you have regular income just handwavium it into your regular income -- fine, agree with that. But the Resource dots cover such a wide range of value it just doesn't make sense to get a free purchase of whatever. I get using it as justification for a Story merit (I invest the treasure into a teahouse/Pay off Lonely Orchid's debts). I get it as fodder for a two-dice stunt on a Bureaucracy roll. I get it for replenishing Resources after a ruinous expense. I get it for handwaving expenses. &quot;You get to make a big buy then it's gone&quot; (especially as described in the OP) just seems tedious to me. Especially when players are reluctant to use it or don't have a clear goal for what they want.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 08-25-2019, 01:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T09:46:46-07:00","id":1332546,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332543#post1332543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Does anyone else find this mildly unsettling?</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, apparently! You and everybody else who ever felt a need to specifically comment upon the inexplicability of either finding themselves in agreement with me or vice versa. Across two forums.<br /> <br /> Anyway, something that I think could be interesting to explore with Resources as it pertains to this game is the possibility that it could be holding some of the Exalted back.<br /> <br /> My observation is that, historically, the primary benefit of being an extremely rich person was that it meant you had a base of resources to accumulate a dedicated labour force that could be focused exclusively on providing services to you. You control all the money and food and you can have craftspeople who can be constantly making fine and delicate clothes and ornaments for every occasion with a constant turnover, you can pour that stuff into throwing lavish parties every other week and support an industry of entertainers, you can even kind of make people into accessories and ornaments by way of ensuring that every room has about five people who'll attend to your every need without you ever needing to look at them or have them moving around in a manner that draws attention.<br /> <br /> It appears to me that industrialisation changed some of the premises of conspicuous consumption for the super-rich, both in the range of luxury items it allowed them to have access to and changing the dynamics of how many actual people were necessary to engage in any given endeavour.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying that such games should be inclined towards industrialisation specifically, but I do think that one could explore interesting characterisation in terms of Exalted who apply their power towards becoming quite rich quite fast and then stagnate for a while because their need to involve themselves is greatly lessened when they can just pay large numbers of people to act as their intermediaries.<br /> <br /> That and the question of getting too comfortable on your laurels in a manner that isn't particularly concerned with where that money is coming from (i.e. the basis of most economies is still going to be agrarian labour), in a manner that might make those systems a good deal more rigid.<br /> <br /> Although, on the other hand, it's not exactly as though late 18th/early 19th century technical developments were driven by desire to liberate the lower classes.... I mean, the seed drill and the cotton gin weren't invented by people committed to relieving the burdens of the working person, they were about increasing commercial output for the people who owned the commodities. Often in ways that came at the expense of people whose livelihoods depended on providing that labour, in a way that ended up funnelling a lot of them into the factories and cities into work and living conditions that were arguably the most horrific they've ever been in human history.<br /> <br /> Anyway, my point is that one might engage with the premises of the Resources Merit and system to interact with these questions and create interesting stories out of them. Not necessarily stories about trying to promote Marxism or the like, mind. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T13:09:25-07:00","id":1332561,"title":"","content":"Was going to mention that as we are in the north it\u2019s not exactly centre of civilisation so barter is the de facto method of trade for most people in a kingdom money usually only comes into play when interacting with merchants and the like . I suspect we are massively overpaying for whatever we are buying with the gems considering th size and purity. Wouldn\u2019t surprise me if this at some point attracted criminal underworld of some sort or the Guild wanting to get hands on it.<br /> <br /> And also I thought it made sense as income as well as the characters wealth comes from the treasure in the largely unexplored ruined ancient city so who knows what other sources of income could be unearthed there. Just charging scholars to safely come and study it could be a source of income nevemind actually setting the citizens to work unearthing more treasure and the like. But considering the way the character is none of that crossed his mind he mostly relies on advice of his adopted Bear father when it comes to life in general and his traveling merchant buddy Jin the Just another lunar who he met when travelling and since he kinda detests civilisation and hates cities and doesn\u2019t really trust people he leaves interactions like that to him. <br /> <br /> His idea of running his kingdom is to ensure everyone is fed protected and has shelter which he achieved back home by subjugating a tribe of man eating white apes using charisma charms and brute strength and using territory charms to make his territory lush and fertile paradise for talking animals to live in.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12796-beast-of-bitter-oblivion","name":"Beast of Bitter Oblivion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12796_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T13:23:54-07:00","id":1332563,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beast of Bitter Oblivion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332561#post1332561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Was going to mention that as we are in the north it\u2019s not exactly centre of civilisation so barter is the de facto method of trade for most people in a kingdom money usually only comes into play when interacting with merchants and the like .</div> </div> </div> </div>It's your table and all, but this is not really reflective of reality or of the setting as portrayed. &quot;Most people in a kingdom&quot; won't resort to barter for their day-to-day transactions; they'll just use money they don't physically have via an elaborate system of credit.<br /> <br /> Especially in the North as-written, communities need to rely on each other to survive; if my neighbor starves because I wouldn't give him any eggs until he could give me something for them, <i>I</i> just made the community weaker.<br /> <br /> \u200bBarter is typically going to happen between strangers or enemies, the kind of people you wouldn't extend credit to because they're not likely to contribute to the overall survival of the community, and/or because they're not likely to come back and pay off their debts in a timely fashion.<br /> <br /> \u200b(So, to be fair, Exalts <i>will</i> often have to resort to barter, but Joe Farmer won't have to get something valuable to trade to his neighbor for assistance in fixing his roof; it'll just be part of the daily tit-for-tat.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T13:48:25-07:00","id":1332566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332563#post1332563\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's your table and all, but this is not really reflective of reality or of the setting as portrayed. &amp;quot;Most people in a kingdom&amp;quot; won't resort to barter for their day-to-day transactions; they'll just use money they don't physically have via an elaborate system of credit.<br /> <br /> Especially in the North as-written, communities need to rely on each other to survive; if my neighbor starves because I wouldn't give him any eggs until he could give me something for them, <i>I</i> just made the community weaker.<br /> <br /> \u200bBarter is typically going to happen between strangers or enemies, the kind of people you wouldn't extend credit to because they're not likely to contribute to the overall survival of the community, and/or because they're not likely to come back and pay off their debts in a timely fashion.<br /> <br /> \u200b(So, to be fair, Exalts <i>will</i> often have to resort to barter, but Joe Farmer won't have to get something valuable to trade to his neighbor for assistance in fixing his roof; it'll just be part of the daily tit-for-tat.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Well barter doesn\u2019t have to be tangible it can be favours as well I was making a point that most people wouldn\u2019t necessarily have physical money.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12796-beast-of-bitter-oblivion","name":"Beast of Bitter Oblivion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12796_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T15:43:53-07:00","id":1332576,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beast of Bitter Oblivion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332566#post1332566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well barter doesn\u2019t have to be tangible it can be favours as well...</div> </div> </div> </div>A favor to be redeemed later is not barter, it's credit; barter necessitates <i>immediate</i> reciprocal exchange.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beast of Bitter Oblivion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332566#post1332566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was making a point that most people wouldn\u2019t necessarily have physical money.</div> </div> </div> </div>And the point I was making is that even in the absence of physical money, both my neighbor and I agree that coins <i>exist</i> (even if we don't have any), and use it as the basis of our exchanges (assuming that we're well-off enough to not just be desperately trying to stay alive, anyway).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 08-25-2019, 06:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-25T16:51:57-07:00","id":1332584,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332576#post1332576\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A favor to be redeemed later is not barter, it's credit; barter necessitates <i>immediate</i> reciprocal exchange.<br /> <br /> And the point I was making is that even in the absence of physical money, both my neighbor and I agree that coins <i>exist</i> (even if we don't have any), and use it as the basis of our exchanges (assuming that we're well-off enough to not just be desperately trying to stay alive, anyway).</div> </div> </div> </div>What\u2019s your point exactly the idea is generally the same one way of the other i don\u2019t understand why the nitpicking ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12796-beast-of-bitter-oblivion","name":"Beast of Bitter Oblivion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12796_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T00:27:29-07:00","id":1332614,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beast of Bitter Oblivion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332584#post1332584\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What\u2019s your point exactly... i don\u2019t understand why the nitpicking ?</div> </div> </div> </div>My point is that the absence of physical money on-hand is not the killer of currency-based transactions that you claimed earlier. The reason that a given Exalt has to barter or have hard money isn't because <i>barter </i>is the norm - it's because the Exalt <b>isn't</b> part of the norm.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beast of Bitter Oblivion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332584#post1332584\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...the idea is generally the same one way of the other...</div> </div> </div> </div>And I happen to feel that the ideas fall very short of similarity. A man building a house for someone might pay for drinks at a bar by plunking down some iron nails, but those nails have an agreed-on value in silver, and he's essentially using them to open a line of credit with the bartender, who knows the builder is going to eventually get paid, so he can come around and collect on his silver. He's not engaging in the act of bartering.<br /> <br /> The Lunar who has to put gemstones on the bar to get served <i>is</i> bartering. The bartender doesn't know him, the Lunar is obviously an outsider; the bartender doesn't know if the Lunar will be here in a year or a month or even tomorrow, let alone working with everyone for the good of the community, and the Lunar doesn't have any coins to trade in, so the bartender is not especially inclined to do business with him at all, let alone give him a favorable deal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 08-26-2019, 03:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T00:45:53-07:00","id":1332616,"title":"","content":"I'm with TheCount on this.<br /> <br /> That said, it's an RPG. If you're happy to gloss over the difference between a throwing knife and a dagger and the system lacks the verisimilitude to differentiate between buying a sword and buying a boat vis cost, then you're probably ok to gloss over the difference between barter and credit.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't. But I also wouldn't gloss over the difference between a longsword, bastard sword and zweihander. I think barter adds immeasurably to the feel of the game and your status as Anathema/wandering heroes and emphasizing the difference between the established community and you. I like the rough edges of silver coins and the feel of cash in hand. To me, it's an important part of the game. But I get that the details which I think make the game POP other people might find tedious or a distraction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T04:19:59-07:00","id":1332632,"title":"","content":"Credit is about trust. It's a very, very different beast from bartering even if both are &quot;exchanges of value&quot;. What reason do you have to trust a complete stranger? In a world where such stranger can totally outrun and outsmart any warning about his deeds?<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T08:15:35-07:00","id":1332651,"title":"","content":"If you feel the need to differentiate that\u2019s up to you my main concern was whether it was largely cashless society or not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12796-beast-of-bitter-oblivion","name":"Beast of Bitter Oblivion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12796_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T08:51:33-07:00","id":1332655,"title":"","content":"Weird or contradictory as it may sound, most people not having cash most of the time does not a cashless society make. They still use it, even if they don\u2019t have it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T10:49:39-07:00","id":1332673,"title":"","content":"I had a notion of continuing to argue in more detail, but\u2026 eh. If you\u2019re playing it how you want to play it, no argument feels effective or worth the effort. I feel as though my perspective on the subject has been thoroughly established by this point.<br /> <br /> So just a few stray thoughts:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Beast just thinks its funny to have a feral Lunar who has all these jewels and has no idea that they're worth so much.</div> </div> </div> </div>I just want to say that these frequent references to a character that is reminiscent of the narrative of the ignorant indigenous person who is sitting unknowingly upon resources valued by more \u201ccivilized\u201d folks who can easily take them for a ride has been making me super uncomfortable, not least with the use of the term \u201cferal\u201d. But I don\u2019t know how it\u2019s being played, maybe the feral person is actually somebody who was raised without human contact and has a number of deep running issues that are treated seriously of which \u201cdoesn\u2019t know what is rare and aesthetically pleasing\u201d is merely the element that intersects with this particular topic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> My point was... who buys 30 suits of lamellar armour, 30 short swords, 30 spears, 30 helmets and 30 shields? And how do you even get it to the shop? Because they're going to find it difficult to shift. Maybe if you can sell them to the local army, but presumably that's the army you just attacked.<br /> If you've got your own army, then pillaging is always popular, but for just, say, 5 PCs, looting the dead isn't going to be all that lucrative in terms of resale value (though they may be carrying cash at least). I mean you'll make some money, but not a ton of Resources.</div> </div> </div> </div>This sends my thoughts off into a tangent about the image of a mere circle of Exalted all on their own being trailed by a group of camp followers, who aren\u2019t strictly affiliated with the circle but have begun trailing in their wake out of a combination of awe and opportunism; that is, they know it\u2019s likely that there\u2019s going to be something worthwhile to clean up after for most of the battles of those Exalted, and maybe even the chance to get in on the ground floor if they found a city or something. People who might occasionally send representatives forging ahead to place tribute before the circle, even if most of the time they\u2019re carefully keeping their distance.<br /> <br /> In any case, second hand merchant buys all of that stuff. They could transport it with, like, two wagons. <br /> <br /> Do people often portray their circles as travelling with a wagon and/or pack animal? Seems like it would be practical. Keychain of Creation had a wagon.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve got this idea of a Lunar guerrilla fighter leading a group of raiders, and the secret to their remarkable high mobility in escape after having approached very carefully and stealthily is that the Lunar turns a bunch of the raiders with him into horses when the time comes to carry all of their plunder away.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just said live off the tax, not, say, hire an army. You don't need very effective tax collection to just live in your big house and not have to do manual labour (well, as long as you seize control of a place that already has a tax collection system, as the PCs generally do).</div> </div> </div> </div>What does seizing control even entail under these circumstances? Have they killed anyone, or do they just roll in with a flag and a gun from the National Rifle Association and assert that everybody works for them now?<br /> <br /> I find the assertion of not needing much effort or accounting to collect taxes or other forms of revenue (often erroneously referred to as taxes) just because it\u2019s a small amount to be rather na\u00efve. Especially in this scenario where the Exalted in question don\u2019t seem to be even offering a veneer of justification for their collections. \u201cGive us a portion of your money so that we can live comfortably\u2026 just because.\u201d Yeah sign me up for that. Governments and landlords have offered the populace a lot more that they\u2019ve alternately resisted paying or gotten up in arms about.<br /> <br /> One thing that\u2019s funny to me in reading about European territorial history that surprisingly doesn\u2019t seem to come up a lot in fiction is the frequency of occasions in which fiefdoms that correspond in many practical respects to the borders and institutions of administrative divisions or even entire nation states are simply\u2026 purchased. Whole countries being something whose ruling dynasties shift (or conflicts between personalities resolved) by exchanging cash and deeds. Stuff that happened as late as the 18th century. We don\u2019t really hear about it much; I feel as though a lot of our conception of history and fiction inspired by it has become so heavily shaped by a world in which the nation state is the norm that we project it back across a history of presumed powerful monarchs, or at least clear pyramidal hierarchies, overlooking just how messy feudal relations made various kinds of territorial division and administrative system.<br /> <br /> It's just interesting to think about the manner in which that keeps certain options off of the table when approaching Exalted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> So they don't actually need any money.</div> </div> </div> </div> Sure they do. It's simply being provided by the largesse of another. It's just them begging without needing to carry the bowl.<br /> <br /> See how little money they need should rich businesswoman stop liking them, or have a sudden downturn that removes the ability to support tenants who don't pay rent, or dies.<br /> <br /> (The world is fiction, so these things can't happen without the command of a Storyteller of course, I just don't care for phrasing it in that manner. There's a difference between not needing money and not needing to <i>work</i>.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T13:41:34-07:00","id":1332719,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332673#post1332673\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I had a notion of continuing to argue in more detail, but\u2026 eh. If you\u2019re playing it how you want to play it, no argument feels effective or worth the effort. I feel as though my perspective on the subject has been thoroughly established by this point.</div> </div> </div> </div>I replied to your other thoughts, but then I realised I'm not sure which bit you don't want to argue about. And sometimes when I'm tired of a conversation, but I see replies, I feel I have to reply.<br /> <br /> So I've put my responses in spoilers, you can just ignore them if you want. Don't feel obligated to reply. Or reply to the bits you find interesting and not the other parts.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> maybe the feral person is actually somebody who was raised without human contact and has a number of deep running issues that are treated seriously of which \u201cdoesn\u2019t know what is rare and aesthetically pleasing\u201d is merely the element that intersects with this particular topic. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, he was literally raised by apes and a talking bear. There's a lot of things he finds weird and confusing about the world of humans.<br /> <br /> He also does know they're aesthetically pleasing, he's just confused why you'd value them more than, say, a cow, which is clearly more useful.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This sends my thoughts off into a tangent about the image of a mere circle of Exalted all on their own being trailed by a group of camp followers, who aren\u2019t strictly affiliated with the circle but have begun trailing in their wake out of a combination of awe and opportunism; that is, they know it\u2019s likely that there\u2019s going to be something worthwhile to clean up after for most of the battles of those Exalted, and maybe even the chance to get in on the ground floor if they found a city or something. People who might occasionally send representatives forging ahead to place tribute before the circle, even if most of the time they\u2019re carefully keeping their distance. </div> </div> </div>Hmmm, that's an interesting idea. Though, while I mentioned that I didn't think it was that profitable to kill enemies, loot their corpses, even besides that I hope most PCs are not slaughtering so many enemies as to attract camp followers to just a band of 5 exalts, because that just seems, well, pretty awful. But I guess it can happen. And that doesn't mean you can't do stuff with the idea.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Do people often portray their circles as travelling with a wagon and/or pack animal? </div> </div> </div>One of the PCs in my Lunar game has a horse and cart. It's been repeatedly very awkward, unfortunately, because it's so slow compared to turning into a pack of wolves, or birds, or going on a boat. Luckily they eventually convinced him to let them take the cart apart to put on the boat, then put it back together when they reach land.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What does seizing control even entail under these circumstances? Have they killed anyone, </div> </div> </div>Generally. Sometimes the ruler so they can replace him with a puppet or themselves, sometimes just people who try and attack them for taking control of the ruler.<br /> <br /> Last session we drove off a sorcerer-king and seized his giant walking fortress, so we didn't kill him, but the Night Caste did kill some guards. Then we threw some minotaurs off the castle to their deaths for sexually abusing their underlings (also the Dawn Caste serving as chief judge, for tyranny and abusing the law, but he didn't die), but we decided to stop until we'd come up with a new legal code that could help the locals resolve the issues of having been members of a brutal military hierarchy with an uncaring ruler for years, allowing abuse to occur in all parts of society, making it hard to judge who's truly guilty. (There was also an argument about whether the law should be based on the subjective beliefs of the locals, or on objective right and wrong. My Zenith monk insisted it should be on objective right and wrong, but the other two judges argued that a man who's taken part in multiple human sacrifices to the Neverborn isn't someone to decide what's objectively right and wrong.)<br /> So, probably next session I'll have to check who's lying and who's not at the trials by the new Truth and Reconciliation commission, but I'm not sure who'll do the killing. Might be us, might be the minotaurs and fox-folk.<br /> <br /> Anyway, so now my monk lives at the top of a giant walking fortress to whom cowed tribes of talking animals offer regular tributes of slaves and food. We turned down the tribute, for now he can just eat gruel with the soldiers, there's a two week supply. This is one of the more moral bands of PCs (shockingly, considering it has a warlord and an assassin), I think the other groups would be happy to just live off the food tribute for a while.<br /> <br /> (Also, this is deep in the wyld in a land of talking animals who thaw out on rising islands then die when they get too close to the sun, so you have to keep moving between islands... anyway, there's no humans besides us, and no money.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I find the assertion of not needing much effort or accounting to collect taxes or other forms of revenue (often erroneously referred to as taxes) just because it\u2019s a small amount to be rather na\u00efve. </div> </div> </div> Oh, someone's doing a lot to collect taxes. Bureaucrats, officials, soldiers. Just not generally the new Solar Prince, King's favourite, King's advisor, or one time the chief inquisitors. (I really felt sorry for the Djala that time.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> One thing that\u2019s funny to me in reading about European territorial history that surprisingly doesn\u2019t seem to come up a lot in fiction is the frequency of occasions in which fiefdoms that correspond in many practical respects to the borders and institutions of administrative divisions or even entire nation states are simply\u2026 purchased. Whole countries being something whose ruling dynasties shift (or conflicts between personalities resolved) by exchanging cash and deeds. Stuff that happened as late as the 18th century. We don\u2019t really hear about it much </div> </div> </div>Well it did come up again recently with Trump asking to buy Greenland the way they bought Alaska. But no, it doesn't come up in fiction, though I think your other point about messy feudal relations is probably more relevant to Exalted (hence the whole &quot;feudalism didn't exist&quot; thing amongst medievalists now).<br /> <br /> I did something with that, with various cities and nomadic tribes who owed fealty to Kings and Queens in different individual ways, but the PCs didn't really investigate it, they weren't that interested. Which did let them get somewhat outmaneuvered by a local Fire-Aspected noble who understood this better than them, but at the end of the day they'd got what they wanted from that kingdom by then anyway, and she couldn't do much against them.<br /> Similarly, there's a very complicated situation with different tribes' views of their relationship with the monarchy and the Eclipse PC who married the aforementioned queen's daughter to legitimise her own rule, which the Eclipse hasn't put much attention into, but then she's trying to completely transform their society anyway, so just tends to ignore any issues about legal views of feudal relations and throw mind control everywhere.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sure they do. It's simply being provided by the largesse of another. It's just them begging without needing to carry the bowl. See how little money they need should rich businesswoman stop liking them, or have a sudden downturn that removes the ability to support tenants who don't pay rent, or dies.<br /> <br /> (The world is fiction, so these things can't happen without the command of a Storyteller of course, I just don't care for phrasing it in that manner. There's a difference between not needing money and not needing to <i>work</i>.) </div> </div> </div>Well, I'm not really disagreeing with you there; my point wasn't that they don't need to think about how they get food, shelter, etc, or that they might not need to work. It's that they don't need to worry about money, specifically, or an official paid job.<br /> <br /> (Actually, there has been a change; my Monk is now living in a sorcerer's tower, the other monk is a slave in the bilges of the ship of a mad warlord. Which sucks for him, but again, he doesn't need cash.)<br /> <br /> I also mentioned Lunars; they can just live in the wilderness if they wish as animals. Obviously a lot of Lunar PCs will want it to buy things; but others won't care. My old Lunar sorcerer either ate food his son-in-law provided, or he just went hunting for mice in his owl form. Sometimes he lived in his son-in-law's house, sometimes he slept in the woods. The only time he needed money was to take his son-in-law to the pub, tbh.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 08-26-2019, 05:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T14:00:45-07:00","id":1332724,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332673#post1332673\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">a combination of awe and opportunism</div> </div> </div> </div>Awepportunism? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> \u200b(I'm so, so sorry.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T14:27:28-07:00","id":1332732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beast of Bitter Oblivion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1332228-how-is-your-party-using-resources?p=1332651#post1332651\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you feel the need to differentiate that\u2019s up to you my main concern was whether it was largely cashless society or not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends how close they are to subsistence.<br /> <br /> Generally speaking, peasant farmers will sometimes have some surplus, and then sell it at a market town for cash. (Whether they have a surplus depends less on the soil than you might think, and more on taxes. Medieval taxes in western Europe on serfs were very, very high. East Germany was lower. Not sure about Russia or Poland) Cash which they'll likely immediately spend in the market town on new tools, shoes, and animals if they can afford it; money doesn't do anything, after all.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, the Inuit didn't used to use cash at all. It is possible to have a cashless society, but it generally is in places with low populations and little surplus.<br /> <br /> (And &quot;cash&quot; can take many forms. The Aztecs used a variety of items as money with discrete values, even including cocoa.)<br /> <br /> <br /> (In the game I run, there's a Danish-esque area of farmers, who might have a little money if the harvest is good; a clan of miners who live underground, who absolutely do need money so they can exchange the metals they sell for food; some trading and fishing towns of ratfolk/lobsterfolk/orcafolk who use money for trade, though your average fisherwoman may not have any money on them most of the time; and an Inuit-esque group out in the further reaches who herd Mammoth. That last one rarely use money, and are more likely to just trade furs and mammoth-ivory straight for iron blades and tools.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 08-26-2019, 05:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}